# The tail both miters share, from a flattened miter module to what write_aiger takes.
# An asynchronous reset becomes one that acts in the cycle it is asserted, the same on
# both sides. A combinational loop stops the run. A used net with no driver is caught
# before this, on each side on its own (cut.ys, gate.ys): miter -flatten has already
# turned any such net into an x, and where it meets an x on the other side the miter's
# own opt_expr folds the comparison to equal, so nothing here would see it. An x left
# after that becomes a free input, so a proof holds for every value it takes. Every flop
# on both sides starts at 0, the RTL's declared initial values included, since the chip
# has none.

async2sync
dffunmap
check -assert
setundef -undriven -anyseq
opt_clean -purge
setattr -unset init
setundef -init -zero
techmap
aigmap
opt_clean -purge
